#  THE 0.2.0 KERNEL'S T-BOX: what `agent/` READS, and nothing more.
#
#  Extracted from the 0.1.0 kernel's ontology (`agent_old/ontology.ttl`) on 2026-09-25 by a census
#  of every `orexis:` term the 0.2.0 tree names in a query text, a term constant or one of its
#  own vocabularies — a mention in prose is not a read — closed over what those declarations
#  reach (a superclass, a domain, a range, a type) and pruned of everything else: the choir, the
#  devices, the identity keys, the obligation, the method and the bridge are the 0.1.0
#  container's and are not here. A boot loads this first, so that a graph's kind is closed over
#  what the vocabulary puts it beneath. A term the tree comes to read is added here; a term
#  nothing reads is annotation and goes.
#
#  The prose on each term is the 0.1.0 file's, kept because it is where the term's meaning was
#  argued; where it names a mechanism the term outlived, the term stays and the prose is a debt
#  to settle when the term is next touched.

@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix planning: <http://example.org/orexis/planning#> .
@prefix execution: <http://example.org/orexis/execution#> .

<> a orexis:OntologyGraph .    # this document is one graph, and this is what it is

#################  Classes  #################

orexis:Action
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Action" ;
    rdfs:comment "One way of acting, WHOLE — the STRIPS operator as one node (knowledge/domain/kernel/action.md), and the KIND of act itself, which an intention commits to and a row carries: `planning:precondition` is a SELECT whose rows are the steps a world admits; `planning:effect` is what taking one makes true and false, as rules; `execution:implementation` is what goes out when one is taken, as operations; `planning:costs` and `planning:landsAfter` sit beside them. Stated by the domain that owns the acting, in its `actions.ttl`, a graph that says it is an `orexis:ActionGraph`, so an author adds a way of acting by adding one node." .

orexis:Agent
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Agent" ;
    rdfs:comment "A self-interested principal: one process, told its own `orexis:localId`, holding one store and acting for a subject (`orexis:actsFor`). What it RUNS is the packages the roles it plays call for (`orexis:plays`); what it can DO is the actions its world's domains ship and those packages take (knowledge/domain/kernel/agent.md)." .

orexis:Arrival
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Arrival" ;
    rdfs:comment "How a graph's content got there. Five values and no sixth: everything in this system is said, computed one of two ways, done, or heard." .

orexis:BeliefGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:label "Belief graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What IS, and so what a possible world is made of: the world as ratified, the vocabulary and what the closure makes of it, the self, what the agent's transitions conclude of what arrives, what is extracted of what was said, a forecast and a committed step. Planning cuts every belief into what some writer of a scope can change and the rest, and reads every world against both. What the agent reads to ACT is no belief: its actions, flows, shapes, constraints, desires, wants and rules are its program, each read by its own kind (#953). There is no kind beneath it for the part that can change: what a plan forks and an effect rewrites is extracted per scope from the writers, the effects, transitions and predictions, as a search's grounds are laid (knowledge/decisions/the-state-is-what-a-scope-can-change-and-everything-known-is-belief.md); and what the kind `orexis:BeliefGraph` said besides is said by provenance, how a graph arrived and whose it is. A graph of this kind the agent DERIVED, and that is no revision, is the agent's own state: what a transition reads beside the arrival that triggers it, and the one place a transition deletes from. An instrument's reading is none: it is sensing's observation, a kind beneath `orexis:Graph` alone, and what the mind reads of it is the subject belief a transition makes (#944)." .

orexis:CatalogueGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:label "Catalogue graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "THE ONE GRAPH THAT SAYS WHAT EVERY GRAPH IS, ITSELF INCLUDED (one-catalogue-describes-every-graph-and-itself): its class, whose it is (`orexis:ownedBy`), how it arrived (`orexis:arrivedBy`), when it holds (`dcterms:temporal`, a `dcterms:PeriodOfTime` either end open) and, for a world's document, the PROV-O account of the load that made it. Written by whoever creates a graph, in the same update as the graph; the vocabulary's own instances below are transcribed into it on every start. It describes ITSELF, which is what lets a store find it without knowing its name — `Store.catalogue` asks every graph for the one that says of itself it is this — so no reader names it, and the three graphs it replaced (the classification, the periods, the provenance) were the bootstrap root's names. NO BELIEF, and the reason is use versus mention: these are statements ABOUT graphs, so merged into the default graph they would answer open patterns that mean something else — `?device a ?class` would return graphs — and a period is a fact that moves, which in every possible world would move the invariant signature and kill a kept cone. The door reads it and hands a rule a dataset; the rule learns nothing of it." .

orexis:Graph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Named graph" .

orexis:OntologyGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:BeliefGraph ;
    rdfs:label "Ontology graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "The T-Box: this module and every other, plus what RDFS entails of it." .

orexis:ActionGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:label "Action graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "A graph of `orexis:Action` rows — what an agent can do, each with what it takes, when it is available, what it costs and what it adds and retracts — named for what it holds, as every graph class is. A domain ships one (`domains/<name>/actions.ttl`), and the planner reads its actions from these graphs alone rather than from every graph, the vocabularies and the world included. No belief: an action is what the agent can do, read by planning and execution to act, and no possible world is made of it; common, since what can be done is everyone's to read. The 0.1.0 kind of this spelling was also a menu graph; 0.2.0 has no menu." .

orexis:RecordGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:label "Record graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "ONE agent's own graph whose period says how long what it states is WORTH BELIEVING, not when it holds (#645): a record of a commitment — a debt, from its issue to the claim's expiry. A reader standing at a later instant is handed such a graph as it stands NOW, since the arrival a standing debt predicts is an arrival at every later instant though the debt's own window will have closed by then; a graph holding DURING its period — a round, a claim, a cooling row, a forecast, a prediction — is handed as of the instant asked about. The class is the door's to read; a package says which of its graphs are records by subclassing it." .

orexis:Role
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Role" ;
    rdfs:comment "The class every role class is beneath (knowledge/domain/kernel/role.md). A role is an INSTANCE an agent plays, never its type: the agent declares it in its own self graph by `orexis:plays` — `:supplier orexis:plays [ a market:Host ]` — and it carries its own context and what belongs to it, as an observer's carries the sensors it reads. A package declares the role classes it serves in its own ontology, beneath this; a domain declares role classes in its own words beneath package ones; and the boot loads a package only where the closure of the classes of the roles the agent plays reaches one its ontology declares. Not beneath `orexis:Agent`: a role is played by an agent and is none. Anti-rigid, as a role is: its player could cease to play it, so no role is every agent's, and an agent playing none loads nothing and is refused at onboarding (knowledge/decisions/a-role-is-an-instance-an-agent-plays.md)." .

orexis:SocietyGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:BeliefGraph ;
    rdfs:label "Society graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "Who a world's principals are and how they reach one another: the agents and the client each is, the brokers as what clients are connected to, the topics and the filters that name them, and which device speaks on which. Sovereign-authored and common, beside the world graph and apart from it, and read by the agents, the simulator and `orexis-mqtt`, which derives the ACL from it. The KERNEL'S kind, since the boot finds who the agent is on it before any package is loaded. Where a broker listens is not here: that is a deployment graph, onboarding's kind, which no agent loads (knowledge/decisions/a-documents-kind-says-who-reads-it.md)." .

orexis:Self
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "Self" ;
    rdfs:comment "The one agent a store is THIS agent's (knowledge/domain/kernel/self.md). AUTHORED by the agent's world, in an `orexis:SelfGraph` of the agent's own, as a desire is — a belief the sovereign gives at birth — and CHECKED by the boot against the one identifier the process is handed, never minted from it. A text asks `?me a orexis:Self` and is bound nothing. A class held to one instance, and never a singleton IRI: the agent keeps the name its world gives it; a self stated anywhere but a self graph, or by a peer or the agent's own saying, is refused, and so is a boot whose own documents state any count of self graphs but one." .

orexis:SelfGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:BeliefGraph ;
    rdfs:label "Self graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which agent is the self, and the one private graph whose owner is read off what it holds rather than stated beside it: `<agent> a orexis:Self`, exactly once, in a document of this kind and no other, authored in the world under `beliefs/` and owned by the self it states. A belief, so a text answered over what is known reads it and it crosses into an imaginarium; OWNED, since it is true of one agent's store and of no other's, so a reader that reads what the world states, the common beliefs, and asks the self states this kind beside them. And where the agent's STANCES are: a figure a package reads of the agent — its patience, a budget, a limit — stated as a triple about the self here and read off this kind alone, the package's constant where none is stated (knowledge/domain/kernel/stance.md). And its ROLES, each an instance the self `orexis:plays` here, read by the boot before any package is loaded (`orexis:Role`)." .

orexis:WorldGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:BeliefGraph ;
    rdfs:label "World graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "The world agents act in: the subjects and their ranges, the sensors, devices and systems, the venues — and, in a world with no society graph beside it, the agents and what each acts for. Sovereign-authored and common, importing the domains the world speaks (knowledge/domain/kernel/world.md)." .

#  WHAT HAPPENED, as the journal keeps it (knowledge/domain/kernel/journal.md): an event a package says, an entry
#  of the agent's journal — a store of its own beside the beliefs, which no part reads — and never a belief.

orexis:Event
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf prov:InstantaneousEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Event" ;
    rdfs:comment "Something a part of an agent says happened, at its `prov:atTime`: an entry of the agent's journal, a named graph holding the event and what the agent held of what it is about, in the words of the package that says it — a plan published, a step taken, a conflict concluded — or a line of its log. Watched and never believed: the journal is a store of its own, and nothing an agent reads is handed it. Packages meet at this word, so the kernel keeps it; each package's events are classes beneath it in its own ontology." .

orexis:Incident
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Incident" ;
    rdfs:comment "An event after which the journal keeps the log before it: the window's records, which it would sweep, are kept for good beside it (`orexis:keptBy`). A package says which of its events are incidents by putting their classes beneath this one — a step that did not land, an intention failed, a want nothing reaches; a world's deployment may count more classes, and says from which level a line of the log is one, WARNING where it says nothing (`onboarding:incident`, `onboarding:incidentLevel`)." .

orexis:LogRecord
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Log record" ;
    rdfs:comment "One line of the agent's log, as the code said it: its logger, its level and its message. Kept for the journal's window and swept after, unless an incident keeps it." .

#################  Properties  #################

orexis:keptBy
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:LogRecord ;
    rdfs:range orexis:Incident ;
    rdfs:label "kept by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The incident a log record is kept for, past the window: it came in the minutes before it." .

orexis:logger
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:LogRecord ;
    rdfs:label "logger" ;
    rdfs:comment "The name of the logger that said it — the package or module of the code." .

orexis:level
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:LogRecord ;
    rdfs:label "level" ;
    rdfs:comment "How loud it was said: DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR or CRITICAL." .

orexis:message
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:LogRecord ;
    rdfs:label "message" ;
    rdfs:comment "What it said, its traceback after it where an exception was logged." .


orexis:actsFor
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "acts for" ;
    rdfs:comment "The subject whose interest this agent advances. Generic; a domain narrows the range — a plant, a tank, a tower." .

orexis:arrivedBy
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:range orexis:Arrival ;
    rdfs:label "arrived by" .

orexis:ownedBy
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:range orexis:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "owned by" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which agent owns this graph — said by the owner in the classification it writes when it creates the graph, beside what the graph is and how it arrived, so a reader that means its OWN graphs of a kind asks by owner as well as by class; and stated by a document the WORLD authors of a graph of an agent's own (`<> orexis:ownedBy :rose_grower`), which is how a boot tells its own documents from another agent's by what they say and never by a file's name. A peer's document and the agent's own saying never state it: the hearer and the sayer are the owner. In a deployed volume every graph is the one agent's; a test standing several agents in one store is kept apart by this, never by a graph's name." .

orexis:end
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain dcterms:PeriodOfTime ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "end" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the period closes, exclusive: an instant equal to this is outside. Absent means the period does not close on its own." .

orexis:hash
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "hash" ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical digest of what a graph holds, on its catalogue row beside what it is — written by whoever lays or forks the graph (a ground, a possible world), and by every writer of a belief or a prediction as it finishes, the boot of every document it put included, and replaced when the graph is re-hashed, since a graph has one content. A belief changes in place under its name, so its row's hash is how a copy made of it knows it is still right, and a test after every world's tests holds every belief and prediction to hash as its row says (a-graph-changes-in-place-and-its-row-says-what-it-holds). Two graphs with one hash hold the same facts whatever their names, which is how a search knows a fork repeated a world it had seen, and how the pass after identifies the present among the worlds the last one imagined: the ground just laid is the world whose hash it repeats, never the one it is named like (the-future-is-a-cone-and-the-present-is-identified-in-it)." .

orexis:localId
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "local id" ;
    rdfs:comment "The short name a resource is known by outside RDF, stated so nothing has to infer it from the shape of a URI: an agent's, the ONE instance identifier a process is given and the string that is also its broker principal, its bucket, its container and its directory — everything else it discovers — and a board's, for the firmware generator. A sensor and a subject need none: the series tags them by the local name of their IRI (agent/sensing/events.py, tag_of), which every world gives them, where a stated id was missing in the one world that mattered (#885)." .

orexis:plays
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ;
    rdfs:range orexis:Role ;
    rdfs:label "plays" ;
    rdfs:comment "A role this agent plays, stated in its own self graph beside `orexis:Self` and holding over that graph's period: an instance of a role class, anonymous unless something must refer to it, carrying its own context. The boot reads it before any package is loaded, so it is a word packages meet at (knowledge/decisions/a-role-is-an-instance-an-agent-plays.md)." .

orexis:start
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain dcterms:PeriodOfTime ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "start" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the period opens. Absent means always, on that side. Ours because DCMI defines no such property: its period is a class with a prose definition and, in the wild, whatever properties its user hangs on it." .

orexis:takes
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:label "takes" ;
    rdfs:comment "A parameter this action is filled with, by IRI. Its local part is the variable its precondition projects, the `$token` its rules read and the predicate a step writes it under. An action may take none — it is then filled with nothing and affords at most one row." .

orexis:Asserted
    a orexis:Arrival ;
    rdfs:label "asserted" ;
    rdfs:comment "The sovereign said so. True by ratification, not by argument." .

orexis:Derived
    a orexis:Arrival ;
    rdfs:label "derived" ;
    rdfs:comment "A package's rule concluded it from given facts. Policy: another society could rule otherwise with the same vocabulary." .

orexis:Received
    a orexis:Arrival ;
    rdfs:label "received" ;
    rdfs:comment "Another party's word: an instrument's reading, a peer's claim. The only arrival whose trustworthiness is a question — an assertion is the sovereign's by definition and a derivation is checkable arithmetic, but this either carries a signature or does not, which is why the graph classification and the trust story are one story." .

orexis:Recorded
    a orexis:Arrival ;
    rdfs:label "recorded" ;
    rdfs:comment "This agent's own act, at runtime. Its word about itself." .

orexis:PlanGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:label "Plan graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "A PLAN PUBLISHED: the steps a search found for one want, in execution's words, written once by planning into the belief base under a name of its own, its root saying which want it `execution:pursues`. Planning owns it and it stays; an intention adopts it by reference (`execution:adopts`). The kernel's because planning writes it and execution reads it, and neither may name the other's word (knowledge/decisions/planning-and-execution-meet-at-the-store.md). No belief: it never crosses into an imaginarium." .

orexis:FlowGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:label "Flow graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "A graph of `prediction:Flow` rows — what moves a value while nobody acts — named for what it holds, as every graph class is. A domain ships one (`domains/<name>/flows.ttl`, `<> a orexis:FlowGraph`), and the prediction package reads its flows from these graphs alone rather than from every graph. No belief, as an action is none: a flow is what prediction reads to foresee, and no possible world is made of it. The KERNEL'S and not prediction's, though the rows are prediction's words: a domain ships its flows whether or not an agent of the world is a predictor, and every agent holds them as a common graph — of a kind only prediction declared, every agent that is no predictor would pass them over and `orexis-onboard` would refuse it, which is refusing a flow with no predictor, foresight being the author's to decline (a-documents-kind-says-who-reads-it, #824, #927). Where a domain's document and the predictor meet is the kernel's to name, as the actions' graph is." .

orexis:ForecastGraph
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:BeliefGraph ;
    rdfs:label "Forecast graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "A FORECAST: anyone's word about what will hold over a stretch ahead, as a graph holding during that stretch — a weather service's hour, which sensing writes from a series; a committed step, the executor's word about its own act over its landing window (`execution:CommittedStepGraph`, beneath this); a peer's route heard. The predictor's own foresight is none: it is the grounds, the flows integrated where each is laid, and writes no graph here (knowledge/domain/prediction/prediction.md, #1000). One kind, since the planner needs one thing of it, that it holds later, and that is a fact about the timeline and not about a class of beings: who said it is provenance, `orexis:arrivedBy` and `prov:wasDerivedFrom`, and finds each author its own (foresight-is-derived-where-the-model-is-the-agents-and-asked-for-where-it-is-anothers, #992). A belief: what the agent holds of a stretch ahead, laid into the ground of its period where its period begins (#987) and never into the present; and the executor answers a step over the readings, never over what was foreseen." .
